Rem ==================================================================
Rem sparky.shoddy — the same dictionary, at a terminal.
Rem
Rem     bin/mill run mills/sparky/sparky.shoddy
Rem     ./build.sh run                            (from this directory)
Rem
Rem THIS IS NOT THE PRODUCT. Sparky is an MCP server and its caller is a
Rem model; this file exists so that the fold can be looked at by a person
Rem without a client in front of them, and so that the mill is a mill
Rem like any other — one that `mill run` runs and `mill weave` weaves.
Rem What it proves is worth having: if the dictionary a client is handed
Rem can be typed at, then the dictionary is real and the server is only a
Rem transport over it.
Rem
Rem Everything that touches the outside world is here and nothing else
Rem is: reading a line, printing what came back, and reading sparkyrc on
Rem the way in. Every decision about what a line MEANS is in
Rem sparky-core.shoddy, which is pure and which test.shoddy grades with
Rem no terminal anywhere near it.
Rem
Rem ---- how you leave ------------------------------------------------
Rem
Rem END OF INPUT, and nothing else: Ctrl-Z then Enter on Windows, Ctrl-D
Rem elsewhere. There is no QUIT and no EXIT, and their absence is the
Rem point rather than an omission. A server has no session to leave, so
Rem sparky-core registers neither — and if this file registered one for
Rem its own convenience, the dictionary a person types at would no longer
Rem be the dictionary a client is handed, which is the one thing this
Rem file exists to demonstrate.
Rem
Rem SAVE, LOAD, TAPESAVE and RESET refuse here for the same reason. They
Rem are the host's words, the server answers them as tools, and this
Rem shell is not that server. They stay registered so WORDS lists them
Rem and HELP explains them, and their bodies say which door does work.
Rem
Rem ---- why the loop is one Def ---------------------------------------
Rem
Rem SpkLoop calls itself and only itself, in tail position. The compiler
Rem turns a SELF tail call into a jump and a mutual one into a call, so a
Rem loop written as SpkLoop -> SpkTurn -> SpkLoop would grow the stack by
Rem a frame per line entered. Gathering an unfinished definition is under
Rem the same rule: the text typed so far is carried as a second argument
Rem rather than recursed into, so three lines of a definition are three
Rem jumps and no frames.
Rem
Rem ---- paths ---------------------------------------------------------
Rem
Rem Resolved against the current directory, and sparkyrc is looked for
Rem there too. Guarded twins throughout — TryReadFile, never ReadFile —
Rem because an unreadable startup file is an ordinary thing to answer and
Rem an aborted mill is not.
Rem ==================================================================

Include "../../machines/str.shoddy"
Include "sparky-core.shoddy"

Rem ---- reading a line --------------------------------------------------

Rem End of input is not an empty line, and here the difference is the
Rem whole way out: InputLine reports the two separately, and this lifts
Rem its flat carrier into an answer that can be matched.
Type SpkIn = SpkEof | SpkTyped(SpkTypedText As String)

Def SpkAsked(prompt As String) As SpkIn
    SpkAskedOf(InputLine(prompt))

Def SpkAskedOf(r As Array) As SpkIn
    If Nth(r, 1) Then
        SpkEof()
    Else
        SpkTyped(Nth(r, 2))

Rem ---- the loop --------------------------------------------------------

Rem What the loop does next, so that reading a line and deciding whether
Rem it is finished both happen OUTSIDE SpkLoop and leave it calling
Rem nothing but itself.
Type SpkStep = SpkGo(GoLine As String) | SpkHold(HoldBuf As String) | SpkStop

Def SpkLoop(st As RckState, buf As String)
    Select Case SpkNext(buf)
        Case SpkGo(line)
            SpkLoop(SpkTurn(st, line), "")
        Case SpkHold(more)
            SpkLoop(st, more)
        Case Else
            SpkDone(st)

Def SpkNext(buf As String) As SpkStep
    SpkStepOf(buf, SpkAsked(SpkPromptFor(buf)))

Def SpkPromptFor(buf As String) As String
    If buf = "" Then
        SpkPrompt()
    Else
        SpkMorePrompt()

Rem END OF INPUT IS THE END, whatever is being gathered: a script that
Rem stops mid-definition closes the session exactly as one that stops
Rem anywhere else does, and the part-built line is dropped rather than
Rem run.
Def SpkStepOf(buf As String, r As SpkIn) As SpkStep
    Select Case r
        Case SpkTyped(line)
            SpkStepJoined(SpkJoined(buf, line))
        Case Else
            SpkStop()

Def SpkStepJoined(j As SpkJoin) As SpkStep
    Select Case j
        Case SpkReady(line)
            SpkGo(line)
        Case SpkMore(more)
            SpkHold(more)
        Case Else
            SpkStop()

Rem Shut whatever the session left open. Not a breach of "nothing is
Rem saved on the way out" — that rule is about not creating things
Rem nobody asked for, and releasing a canvas the session explicitly
Rem opened is the opposite. It is also what lets the mill exit: the
Rem window pump keeps going while any scribbler is open.
Def SpkDone(st As RckState)
    RckShutAll(st)
    Print("")

Rem One turn: evaluate, show it, and hand back the state to go on from.
Rem A refusal is a state too — the one the line started from, with the
Rem refusal on the tape.
Def SpkTurn(st As RckState, line As String) As RckState
    SpkShowed(st, SpkDo(st, line))

Def SpkShowed(st As RckState, r As RckLine) As RckState
    Each(SpkShown(r), Print)
    SpkAfter(st, r)

Rem ---- starting up -----------------------------------------------------

Rem A missing sparkyrc is silence, not an error: most sessions have no
Rem such file and a mill that complained about its absence would be
Rem complaining at everyone. A file that IS there and will not load says
Rem so and starts anyway, because a bad startup file is a reason to fix
Rem the file and not a reason to have no dictionary.
Rem
Rem It loads through RckLoad rather than through a turn, so it leaves no
Rem tape rows: the tape is a record of a session, and nobody typed this.
Def SpkStart(st As RckState) As RckState
    If Not FileExists(SpkRcName()) Then
        st
    Else
        SpkStartRead(st, TryReadFile(SpkRcName()))

Def SpkStartRead(st As RckState, r As Result) As RckState
    Select Case r
        Case Ok(text)
            SpkStartLoad(st, RckLoad(st, SpkTextLines(text)))
        Case Else
            SpkStartSaid(st, SpkRcName() & ": cannot read it")

Def SpkStartLoad(st As RckState, r As RckLine) As RckState
    Select Case r
        Case RckNext(st2, out)
            SpkStartSaid(st2, SpkRcName() & ": loaded " & SpkCounted(SpkUserCount(st2) - SpkUserCount(st), "word", "words"))
        Case RckStop(st2, why, out)
            SpkStartSaid(st, SpkRcName() & ": " & why)
        Case Else
            st

Def SpkStartSaid(st As RckState, said As String) As RckState
    Print(said)
    st

Rem Split on the newline and Trim what is left, which takes the carriage
Rem return off a file written on Windows and read on anything else.
Def SpkTextLines(text As String) As List Of String
    Map(Split(text, Chr(10)), Trim)

Rem ---- the run ---------------------------------------------------------

Rem The line about leaving belongs to this file and not to SpkBanner:
Rem the banner is carried into the server's grounding as it stands, and
Rem a client told to press Ctrl-Z would be told something false.
Def SpkConsoleNote() As String
    "This is the server's dictionary at a terminal. End input (Ctrl-Z, or Ctrl-D) to leave."

Def SpkOpened() As RckState
    Let st = SpkStart(SpkFresh())
    Each(RckShow(st), Print)
    st

Def Main()
    Each(SpkBanner(), Print)
    Print(SpkConsoleNote())
    SpkLoop(SpkOpened(), "")
